>
  <h2>What "cheapest" usually leaves out</h2>
  <p>
    A virtual office address has two jobs. One is the business address you put on your website, invoices and letters. The other is the
    registered office, the official address Companies House holds for your company. Many of the cheapest prices cover the first job only.
    Before you compare monthly prices, check which of these are in the price and which are extra:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The registered office address.</strong> Often a separate plan or a yearly fee on top of a "business address" price.</li>
    <li><strong>A director's service address.</strong> The address shown against you as a director. Without it, your home can stay on the public register even after the company's registered office changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Post.</strong> Some plans only receive official post from Companies House and HMRC. Forwarding other post is charged per item, and scanning is a higher plan.</li>
    <li><strong>A setup fee.</strong> A one-off charge of £20 to £50 is common and rarely in the headline.</li>
    <li><strong>An identity check fee.</strong> Providers must carry out anti-money laundering checks; a few charge for them.</li>
    <li><strong>VAT.</strong> "From £10" is sometimes £12 once VAT is added. Check whether the price shown includes it.</li>
    <li><strong>The contract.</strong> The lowest monthly rate is often a year paid in advance. A monthly rolling plan can cost a little more each month and much less if you only need it for six.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a cheap address must still be</h2>
  <p>Whatever the price, Companies House has the same rules for a registered office:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>An appropriate address.</strong> Post sent to the company would come to the attention of someone acting for it, and delivery could be acknowledged.</li>
    <li><strong>A physical address, not a PO box.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>In the right part of the UK.</strong> A company registered in England and Wales needs a registered office in England or Wales; Scottish and Northern Irish companies need one in Scotland or Northern Ireland.</li>
    <li><strong>With the provider's permission</strong>, which comes with your agreement and their anti-money laundering checks on you.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A staffed building where real people take in the post meets all four. That is the test to apply to any price that looks too good:
    who, exactly, picks up the letter from HMRC?
  </p>

