  <h2>How to compare prices properly</h2>
  <p>Put every provider through the same six questions, and write the answers in a row each. The cheapest row at the end is rarely the cheapest headline.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Is the registered office address included, or a separate fee?</li>
    <li>Is a director's service address included, and what does it add?</li>
    <li>What happens to post: collected, forwarded per item, or scanned? What does a scanned letter cost?</li>
    <li>Is there a setup fee, an identity check fee, or VAT on top?</li>
    <li>What is the minimum term, and can you cancel monthly?</li>
    <li>Is it a staffed building you could visit, or a mailbox at a sorting address?</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>When the cheapest is not the best value</h2>
  <p>
    Official letters go to your registered office: Companies House reminders, HMRC penalties, court papers. The cost of one missed letter
    is more than a year's difference between a £10 plan and a £20 one. So weigh the price against who handles the post, how quickly you
    are told it has arrived, and whether you can collect it. A provider you can phone and visit is worth a few pounds a month.
  </p>
